They appear to come from Brazillian ISPs.  I say "appear"
because... I googled it, thinking it was going to be like
one of those crappy crawlers that click on every link
(for instance recently I read about
&lt;https://zadzmo.org/blog/posts/2025/1218-Thinkbot.md&gt;
&lt;https://boston.conman.org/2025/08/21.1&gt;)  But
the truth is MY SERVER may have been used to attack
them instead.  Basically, post and relevant reply:

&lt;https://www.reddit.com/r/sysadmin/comments/1r8m3oq/&gt;
&lt;https://www.reddit.com/r/sysadmin/comments/1r8m3oq/comment/o6f07ty/&gt;

(This case of that is definitely different in that these
"dhl.com IN ANY" requests are made over UDP (EDNS0, to be
exact); but the general premise is there.)

And a lot more when I google "reflection attack" (not so
much "amplification" though):

&lt;https://www.netscout.com/what-is-ddos/what-is-reflection-amplification-attack&gt;
&lt;https://docs.aws.amazon.com/whitepapers/latest/aws-best-practices-ddos-resiliency/udp-reflection-attacks.html&gt;
&lt;https://www.cloudflare.com/learning/ddos/dns-amplification-ddos-attack/&gt;
&lt;https://security.stackexchange.com/a/181123&gt;
&lt;https://security.stackexchange.com/a/94270&gt;
&lt;https://www.imperva.com/learn/ddos/dns-amplification/&gt;

So the flow of such an attack goes like this:

  * Botnet sends a plausible UDP packet, saying "I want
    *every* record---A, AAAA, TXT, and all that---at the
    name dhl.com.", to a DNS resolver, and forges a source
    address pointing to one of the addresses of Brazillian
    ISP.  (That is VPSes, but possibly residential IP too.)

  * The DNS resolver says "ok" and sends back to the forged
    address, with kilobytes of (unsolicited) UDP packet.

  * Do this many many times, appearing as many many
    Brazillian IPs.  Each DNS request is 40-80 bytes
    (source: claude; I don't really know how large a
    DNS packet is lol, it's less than 256 for sure),
    but the response gets amplified to 2-6 kilobytes.
    Maximal damage for minimal effort!

Wikipedia has a neat diagram for it (although the gray
mess representing "the Internet" is kind of funny, lol):
&lt;https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/IP_address_spoofing&gt;

(Although, now that I checked against my own recursive
resolvers, the TCP "rcvd" size is between 6888 and 6063,
which are both too big for BIND9's default max-udp-size and
edns-udp-size, so it doesn't matter how large the attacker
advertises their +bufsize in EDNS (even if they crank it all
the way up to the theoretical maximum UDP size of "65535"!).
In practice UDP is a dead-end; though they may succeed with
TCP.)

But in a sense I can't do anything about it: block these
AS ranges and it would have achieved one possible intent of
these attackers: cripple Internet service for Brazillian
residents.  Such an attack leaves no trace either, because
IP/UDP/TCP is like, a very low level protocol... (the fact
that these packets even got far is a different concern
though; suffice it to say though when my server receives
them it is indisginguishable from a legitimiate DNS query,
albeit suspicious, most obviously to me because of dhl.com
(Why the hell would you think my servers are authoritative
for those guys?  (Or they may not care that my server is
not a public recursive resolver like Cloudflare/Google are;
they did a rudimentary port scan and found an open port 53,
so it bluntly assumes this would be one of those guys.))
but also because it is an ANY-type query, which I have
previously not paid any attention to (nor have the awareness
of---I mean, there is TONS of DNS records I have never even
heard of before: SSHFP, OPENPGPKEY, SVCB (not SRV!), HTTPS;
and there's tons more deprecated.  No way am I going to pay
attention to all of them!)  But ANY is a special type that
literally means "return any record type"; a wildcard, if
you will.  And that's... not the best, yeah.  (I mean I
love it and would like that as an alternative to AXFR; but
for the world?  Especially when botnet tricks my server
into dumping an entire zone on innocent IPs?  No thanks...)

Fortunately, BIND9 has a config for that!

&lt;https://bind9.readthedocs.io/en/stable/reference.html#namedconf-statement-minimal-any&gt;

so before the attacker could figure out what zone I am
really authoritative for and use that to attack, I can
"disable" ANY queries... the issue is I haven't been able
to get it working for the time being so I'll have to go
figure that out now.
